en.help.message:
slangd is the Slang shader language server (diagnostics, completion, hover and go-to-definition for .slang files); slangc is the compiler beside it.

Install and configure:
  * download a Slang binary release from https://github.com/shader-slang/slang/releases and put both slangc and slangd on PATH (Slang has no single official package-manager command, so Copy Install Command copies that release instruction);
  * add a language-server entry for Slang in config.json (lsp.slang with command slangd; if gvim does not detect .slang files as a Slang language yet, add a languages.slang entry with the .slang/.slangh extensions), then run Start/Restart LSP Server after changing it;
  * verify both tools with Slang: Check slangd / slangc; a start error is reported verbatim (it may be a missing binary, a remote-authority error or a sandbox refusal) and is never rewritten as not found.

Builtin modules (slang-synth://):
  * slangd answers go-to-definition on builtins (float3, normalize, ...) with a synthetic URI such as slang-synth://core/core.builtin that has no file on disk;
  * Fresh's slang-lsp.ts claims that URI scheme and dumps the requested module with slangd --print-builtin-module <module> into a cached .slang file, then opens it read-only;
  * gvim's plugin API has no LSP URI-scheme/external-URI hook, so this port cannot open those targets; the editor keeps its external-location fallback. The mechanism and its implementation guide are documented upstream.

Docs: https://shader-slang.org/docs/  (language server and tooling pages)
